# PostgreSQL Host-Based Authentication File (pg_hba.conf)
#
# This file controls which clients are allowed to connect to the database.
# Each line defines a rule in the following format:
#
# TYPE  DATABASE        USER            ADDRESS                 METHOD
#
# TYPE:
#   local   - Unix domain socket connections (local only)
#   host    - TCP/IP connections (remote or local)
#   hostssl - TCP/IP connections requiring SSL
#   hostnossl - TCP/IP connections without SSL
#
# DATABASE:
#   all             - all databases
#   sameuser        - databases with the same name as the user
#   replication     - replication connections
#   (comma-separated list or database name)
#
# USER:
#   all             - all users
#   (comma-separated list or username)
#   +group          - members of a role group
#
# ADDRESS (for host types):
#   127.0.0.1/32    - localhost IPv4
#   ::1/128         - localhost IPv6
#   0.0.0.0/0       - all IPv4 addresses
#   ::/0            - all IPv6 addresses
#
# METHOD:
#   trust           - allow without password (DANGEROUS, for testing only)
#   reject          - reject connection
#   md5             - password authentication (MD5 hashed)
#   scram-sha-256   - password authentication (SCRAM, recommended)
#   peer            - OS username must match database username (local only)
#   ident           - use IDENT protocol (not recommended)
#
# Rules are processed top-to-bottom. The first match wins.

# ============================================================
# Local connections (Unix socket)
# ============================================================

# Superuser can connect locally without a password via peer auth
local   all             postgres                                peer

# Other local users connect via peer auth
local   all             all                                     peer

# ============================================================
# TCP/IP connections
# ============================================================

# Allow localhost connections with password authentication
host    all             all             127.0.0.1/32            scram-sha-256
host    all             all             ::1/128                 scram-sha-256

# ============================================================
# Practice exercises
# ============================================================
#
# 1. Change the authentication method from scram-sha-256 to md5 and restart.
#    Notice the difference in how passwords are stored.
#
# 2. Add a rule to allow connections from a specific subnet:
#    host    all         all         192.168.1.0/24          scram-sha-256
#
# 3. Add a rule to reject connections from a specific IP:
#    host    all         all         10.0.0.5/32             reject
#
# 4. Create a rule that allows a specific user to connect without a password
#    (trust method) — then test it and remove it (security risk!).
#
# 5. Restrict a user to only connect to a specific database:
#    host    practice_db lpic_student  127.0.0.1/32          scram-sha-256
